 \begin{abstract}
-An AI agent's memory stores differ in the structure they already record. Logs number their lines, example banks separate their records, and prose does neither. RAG keeps every word and can score well after heavy tuning to a dataset, but the tuning does not generalize. Model-built memory scales to long histories but is costly to build and can drop evidence. Agents need an autonomous strategy that is cheap to build and leaves little to tune. \sys{} detects each store's structure and builds memory from it. Two byte statistics, the shares of numbered lines and of text in records short enough to keep whole, set how a store is cut, grouped and ordered, with no model, labels, queries or candidate runs. \sys{} matches retrieval to that structure, which sets how dense, lexical and parent-graph channels split the slots. It assembles the context in stored order, keeping the newest version of each fact where an order is recorded. One rule, set on MemoryAgentBench, configures LoCoMo and BEAM unchanged. On MemoryAgentBench it is the strongest retrieval and memory system at the benchmark's reader ($57.5$ against $43.6$). On LoCoMo it is the strongest row under both answer prompts ($86.04$ and $93.46$) with no generative call before the answer, where the strongest other system makes $9{,}398$. On LoCoMo-Refined it matches that system under the official evaluation ($77.57$ against $75.33$) and leads it by $8.5$ points with a reasoning reader. On BEAM, from 100K to 10M tokens, it matches the strongest system we compare at equal context and has the highest mean with an open-weight reader at 9.7K tokens. It builds a 10M-token memory in $77$ seconds, where model-built memories take hours. Read beside a model-built memory, it averages $73.1$ on BEAM at $19.5$K tokens, above either store alone and every system at that budget. \sys{} keeps each store as written and re-derives its configuration in seconds as the store grows.
