 The memory systems we run are reported on conversation benchmarks alone, and their lead does not carry. The strongest other system we ran on LoCoMo scores $23.1$ on MemoryAgentBench, below BM25's $41.5$ (Table~\ref{tab:board}). \sys{} is set on MemoryAgentBench, and its rule configures LoCoMo, LoCoMo-Refined, BEAM and a model-built memory automatically from their bytes, with no change. In the results of Section~\ref{sec:results}, \sys{} is the strongest memory system on each benchmark or matches it at equal context.
 
-\paragraph{Detect before you construct.} Unlike True Memory \cite{truememory}, which reads every store with one fixed representation, and Adaptive Chunking \cite{adaptivechunk}, which picks a chunker by running every candidate, \sys{} derives a store's unit, parent, order and retrieval policy from two byte statistics, with no model and no candidate run. It \emph{detects} whether the store records the structure a reader needs, including an order, and sets the unit and hierarchy from it. It \emph{matches} a hybrid retrieval to that structure, giving the parent graph most slots on a numbered log and balancing it with dense and lexical search elsewhere. It \emph{assembles} the context in stored order, keeping the newest version of a numbered fact. An order the store does not record is worse than none. On fixed selections, shuffled serials score $59$ on one fact-consolidation cell, below $64$ with no order and $92$ with the recorded one (Appendix~\ref{app:slotsrc}). Deterministic conflict resolution \cite{freshness} assumes the serial from the task format and extracts facts with a model on every query, while \sys{} detects the serial and scores $92.0$ and $37.0$ on the fact-consolidation cells against the $82.0$ and $27.0$ it reports at the same reader.
+\paragraph{Detect before you construct.} Unlike True Memory \cite{truememory}, which reads every store with one fixed representation, and Adaptive Chunking \cite{adaptivechunk}, which picks a chunker by running every candidate, \sys{} derives a store's unit, parent, order and retrieval policy from two byte statistics, with no model and no candidate run. It \emph{detects} whether the store records the structure a reader needs, including an order, and sets the unit and hierarchy from it. It \emph{matches} a hybrid retrieval to that structure, giving the parent graph most slots on a numbered log and balancing it with dense and lexical search elsewhere. It \emph{assembles} the context in stored order, keeping the newest version of a numbered fact. An order the store does not record is worse than none. On fixed selections, shuffled serials score $59$ on one fact-consolidation cell, below $64$ with no order and $92$ with the recorded one (Appendix~\ref{app:slotsrc}). Deterministic conflict resolution \cite{freshness} assumes the serial from the task format and extracts facts with a model on every query, while \sys{} detects the serial and scores $93.0$ and $60.0$ on the fact-consolidation cells with the released implementation ($92.0$ and $37.0$ in the original), against the $82.0$ and $27.0$ it reports at the same reader.
 
 \paragraph{Contributions.}
 \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=*,nosep]
 \item \textbf{Detection before construction, for different kinds of agent memory.}
 \sys{} configures a store's read path from two closed-form statistics of its bytes, with no model, labels, per-store queries or candidate runs. It re-derives that configuration in seconds as the store grows. The share of numbered lines tells whether the store records an order and only then turns on keep-newest resolution and parent-priority retrieval. The share of text in short records tells whether a finer cut keeps every record whole. The detected structure then sets how hybrid retrieval splits the slots among its channels, and assembly keeps stored order.
 \item \textbf{First place on MemoryAgentBench.}
-\sys{} ranks first overall among the retrieval and memory systems at the benchmark's reader, $57.5$ against $43.6$, with its hybrid retrieval and assembly alone at $50.3$ (Table~\ref{tab:gate}), and first on every competency among the retrieval and memory systems the benchmark reports, rising to $60.9$ and $64.7$ with GPT-4.1-mini and GPT-5-mini.\looseness=-1
+The released implementation with the paper settings scores $60.5$ against $43.6$ at the benchmark's GPT-4o-mini reader and has the highest Overall among the retrieval and memory systems in Table~\ref{tab:board} at that reader. It scores $62.1$ and $67.0$ with GPT-4.1-mini and GPT-5-mini, and $69.7$ and $67.0$ with GLM-5.3-Flash and GPT-6 Luna. Per-store automatic configuration scores $58.5$ at GPT-4o-mini. Table~\ref{tab:board} retains the original implementation for comparison.\looseness=-1
